63,3,,status,Mizuki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
64,3,,status,Yumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
65,3,1,talk,Rin,,"Wait, Option A sounds like a nightmare! If we have to plug in and set up screens every single morning, we'll waste so much time! Plus, the early birds would get all the best spots!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option A is financially reckless. The constant movement would likely increase wear and tear, and it doesn't solve our issue of paying for empty space. It is an unstable solution for a team with a tight budget.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A is a recipe for conflict. If the best seats are first-come, first-served, we're just rewarding arrival times rather than making a fair decision. It ignores the daylight and noise issues entirely.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,1,talk,Shion,,"Option A is an absurdity. Given that it takes days of fine-tuning to make a workstation physically viable, forcing us to reset our ergonomics daily is a recipe for chronic pain. It's a total disregard for the body.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"I agree with everyone! Option A sounds so lonely and stressful. We wouldn't have a ""home"" together, and it would be so hard to stay connected and help each other if we're always moving!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Rin,,"@Toshio, @Mizuki, @Shion, @Yumi, since we all hate Option A, what about B or C? Option B sounds like we'd have to move everything again and again, which sounds like a lot of wasted time!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Mizuki"",""Shion"",""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Toshio,,Option B is a financial disaster. Every shift would incur the facilities charge for re-running power and network. We cannot justify repeated expenditures for a rotating system when our budget is already strained.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"Option B is a superficial fix for fairness. While it rotates the ""bad"" seats, it guarantees the same disruptive shuffles I found in the records. It's an inefficient way to handle the window and noise disparity.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,2,talk,Shion,,Option B is just a slow-motion version of Option A. It forces us to abandon a perfectly calibrated workstation just as it becomes comfortable. It's a logical failure that prioritizes a facade of fairness over actual physical stability.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Rin, I agree! Moving all the time sounds so tiring. Plus, if we keep shifting, we can't really build a cozy little community in our space. I think we should look at Option C instead!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Rin,,I agree with @Yumi! Option C sounds the best because we only set up once and then we're done! No more wasting time every morning or moving around. It's the fastest and happiest way!,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"Option C is the only fiscally responsible choice. It minimizes the facilities charges to a single initial cost and allows us to optimize the billed area without the risk of repeated, unplanned expenditures.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"Option C is the most logical, but we must be explicit about the assignments. Since some seats are objectively worse, we should decide now who takes them so there's no resentment later. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,3,talk,Shion,,"Option C is the only choice that doesn't treat our bodies as disposable. It allows for the precise, long-term calibration required for actual comfort. I vote for Option C, provided the assignments are based on physical needs.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,3,talk,Yumi,,"I'm so happy we all agree! Option C lets us stay close, help each other, and feel like a real team in our own little home. I vote for Option C!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Rin,,I vote for Option C too! It's the best way to save our time and keep everyone smiling. Let's make our new spots super cozy! Option C it is!,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"I vote for Option C. It is the only choice that ensures financial stability and prevents wasteful spending. Let us finalize this and move forward with a precise, cost-effective plan.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I've already cast my vote for Option C. Now that we've agreed, let's be honest about the seat quality and decide the assignments together so that no one feels overlooked or unfairly treated.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
83,3,4,talk,Shion,,"I vote for Option C. Let's just ensure that ""fairness"" doesn't mean ignoring the noise and ergonomics. I'll be expecting a layout that respects the physical reality of the room.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
84,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I vote for Option C! I'm so glad we're all on the same page. Let's make sure we sit in a way that keeps us all connected and happy!,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
